STTY(1)                    UNIX Programmer's Manual                    STTY(1)

NNAAMMEE
       stty - set and report terminal modes

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ssttttyy [--aa | --ee] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [_o_p_e_r_a_n_d ...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       _s_t_t_y sets and reports the I/O modes of a terminal, writing its report
       to the diagnostic output.  With no operand it reports the terminal
       speed and the mode bits that differ from their defaults; with --aa it
       reports everything it knows.  It operates on the standard output unless
       --ff names another terminal.

       On FGX the terminal is the old-style _s_g_t_t_y(3) interface: the ssggttttyybb
       mode word (below), the ttcchhaarrss special characters, and a read-only
       window size.  The following options are available:

       --aa      Report every mode _s_t_t_y knows.  Equivalent to the operand aallll or
               eevveerryytthhiinngg.

       --ee      Same as --aa.

       --ff _f_i_l_e Open and use the terminal named by _f_i_l_e rather than the
               standard output -- for example ssttttyy --ff //ddeevv//ttttyy0000 to inspect
               the second serial port.

       The operands aallll and eevveerryytthhiinngg report everything (as --aa); ssppeeeedd prints
       the terminal speed alone and ssiizzee prints the window size (rows then
       columns) on the standard output.

MMOODDEESS
       The mode operands set or clear ssgg__ffllaaggss bits.  Each has a negated form
       spelled with a leading --.

       rraaww       Raw mode: no input processing (erase, kill, interrupt); each
                 byte is delivered to _r_e_a_d(2) as received.  --rraaww (or ccooookkeedd)
                 restores line processing.

       ccbbrreeaakk    Deliver each character to _r_e_a_d as received, but keep
                 interrupt processing; no erase/kill.  --ccbbrreeaakk delivers input
                 only a line at a time.

       eecchhoo      Echo input characters.  --eecchhoo suppresses the echo (as _v_i does
                 in visual mode).

       nnll        Accept only newline to end a line.  --nnll allows carriage
                 return for newline and maps output accordingly.

       ttaabbss      Preserve tabs on output.  --ttaabbss expands tabs to spaces.

       ttaannddeemm    Enable xon/xoff (start/stop) input flow control; --ttaannddeemm
                 disables it.

SSPPEECCIIAALL CCHHAARRAACCTTEERRSS
       For the operands that take a character argument _c, _c may be given
       literally, as ``uunnddeeff'' to leave the character undefined, or as a two-
       character ^^_x sequence naming a control character (^^??= delete).

       eerraassee _c   Erase (backspace) character (default DEL, ^?).

       kkiillll _c    Line-kill character (default ^U).

       iinnttrr _c    Interrupt character (default ^C).

       qquuiitt _c    Quit character.

       ssttaarrtt _c   Output-start character (default ^Q).

       ssttoopp _c    Output-stop character (default ^S).

       eeooff _c     End-of-file character (default ^D).

       bbrrkk _c     Additional line-delimiter (wakeup) character; undefined by
                 default.

SSPPEEEEDD
       00 5500 7755 111100 113344 115500 220000 330000 660000 11220000 11880000 22440000 44880000 99660000 1199220000 3388440000
       Record the terminal input and output speed as the number given.  The
       console boots at 9600 baud.

FFGGXX NNOOTTEESS
       FGX's console line discipline is 2.11BSD's, so it acts on EECCHHOO,
       CCBBRREEAAKK, RRAAWW (single-keystroke unechoed input for _v_i), CCRRMMOODD,
       XXTTAABBSS and TTAANNDDEEMM.  The ttcchhaarrss characters are LIVE: the interrupt,
       quit, start and stop characters are the ones the discipline compares
       each arriving keystroke against, so ssttttyy  iinnttrr really does move ^C.
       They used to be merely saved and restored; the tty campaign changed
       that.

       The window size is REPORTED per line and is not settable here; there is
       no rroowwss or ccoollss operand.  Each tty carries its own size and a pty
       tracks what its far end reports, so the figure is a live reading of the
       session it is run in.

       The LOCAL MODES are accepted: ccrrttbbss, ccrrtteerraassee, ccrrttkkiillll, pprrtteerraassee,
       ccttlleecchhoo, lliittoouutt, ppaassss88, ttoossttoopp, mmddmmbbuuff, nnoohhaanngg, rrttssccttss,
       ddeeccccttllqq and nnooffllsshh, each with a leading `-' to clear it, plus the
       compounds ccrrtt, nneewwccrrtt and ddeecc.  ccttlleecchhoo is the one that makes a
       typed control character echo as ^X: without it the discipline emits the
       raw byte and a ^^CC leaves no mark on the screen.  ddeecc is the 2.11BSD
       idiom, setting ccrrttbbss, ccttlleecchhoo and ddeeccccttllqq together with the DEC
       erase/kill/interrupt characters.

       Terminal features FGX's console model does not provide are still not
       accepted: parity (eevveenn/oodddd), line disciplines (nneeww/oolldd), the
       llttcchhaarrss group (ssuusspp, wweerraassee) and modem control (ddccdd, ddttrr,
       fflluusshhoouutt, hhuupp).

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
       _s_g_t_t_y(3), _i_o_c_t_l(2), _v_i(1), _t_s_e_t(1)

AAUUTTHHOORR
       Ported to FGX from the 4BSD/2.11BSD _s_t_t_y, scoped to the FGX _s_g_t_t_y
       terminal interface.

